WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.072
One point two three two trillion dollars.

00:00:03.022 --> 00:00:07.363
That is Canada's federal market debt for 2024-25.

00:00:07.363 --> 00:00:14.352
Annual debt servicing: fifty-four point one billion dollars — approximately one hundred and fifty million dollars per day.

00:00:14.352 --> 00:00:19.875
That single line item exceeds the entire Canada Health Transfer of fifty-two point one billion.

00:00:19.875 --> 00:00:24.829
The government spends more on interest payments than it transfers to provinces for healthcare.

00:00:24.829 --> 00:00:28.795
Debt per capita exceeds thirty-one thousand four hundred dollars.

00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:36.818
Debt-to-GDP: forty-two point seven percent federal, one hundred and seven percent total government including provinces per the IMF.

00:00:36.818 --> 00:00:43.829
Fifty-four billion dollars could build two hundred and seventy hospitals or fund one point eight million affordable housing units.

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:50.954
Instead, it services debt accumulated through decades of structural deficits that persisted even through periods of economic expansion.

00:00:50.954 --> 00:00:53.988
The debt is not a crisis that happened to Canada.

00:00:53.988 --> 00:00:56.443
It is a crisis Canada chose.
